    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mb-4">
        <code>ComposerTemplate</code> replaces the built-in composer with host markup — here a fan
        view that gates replies behind a subscription. The host registers its textarea with
        <code>context.AttachAsync(input)</code> once mounted, so Enter/IME and the refocus behave
        exactly like the built-in composer; <code>context.SetTextAsync</code> keeps the draft and
        <code>context.SendAsync</code> goes through the same guarded pipeline.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div class="showcase-card" style="height: 440px;">
            <TmChat @ref="_gateChat"
                    CurrentUser="_gateFan"
                    Messages="_gateMessages"
                    MessageTemplate="RenderGateMessage"
                    UseTemplateFor="@(m => m.Kind is not null)"
                    OnSendMessage="HandleGateSend"
                    ComposerTemplate="SubscribeGateComposer"
                    MessagesAriaLabel="Subscriber chat messages" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 self-start">
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
                The gate is plain host markup: until you subscribe, the chat renders a subscribe
                card instead of an input (and the draft guard still applies to the host composer).
                Press <em>Subscribe</em> — the composer mounts, the Enter/IME pipeline attaches and
                Dana answers a moment after your first reply. Dana's paid voice note is locked:
                press <em>Unlock</em> to pay $9.99 and reveal it as a normal row.
            </p>
            <TmButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary" Size="ButtonSize.Sm" OnClick="HandleGateReset">
                Reset subscription
            </TmButton>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- Auto-Attach (FZE-12): the template input carries id=InputId and TmChat attaches it itself -->
<section class="demo-section" data-testid="chat-autoattach-section">
    <div class="flex items-center space-x-2 mb-4">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Auto-Attached Composer</h2>
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium bg-pink-100 dark:bg-pink-900/30 text-pink-700 dark:text-pink-300 rounded-full">FZE-12</span>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mb-4">
        The same <code>ComposerTemplate</code> contract WITHOUT the host-side
        <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> + <code>AttachAsync</code> plumbing: the template's
        textarea carries <code>id="@("@composer.InputId")"</code> and
        <code>AutoAttachComposerInput="true"</code> makes TmChat resolve and attach it by id
        (scoped to the chat root). Enter sends, Shift+Enter inserts a new line, an IME
        composition never submits, the textarea auto-resizes and the focus returns to it after
        every send — the JS module owns it all. An explicit <code>context.AttachAsync(element)</code>
        would always win; calling it with <code>default</code> hands the control back.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
